<header class="sp-head"><a class="sp-portrait" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc.jpg" alt="" width="104" height="104"></a><p class="featured-region">24 February 1431 · Rouen</p><h1 class="sp-title">Joan of Arc before Her Judges</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Two years after she had helped lift the English siege of Orléans and seen Charles VII crowned at Reims, Joan of Arc was captured by Burgundian troops at Compiègne and sold to the English. At Rouen a church court led by Bishop Pierre Cauchon tried her for heresy, questioning the nineteen-year-old for weeks in the hope of trapping her. Asked whether she knew herself to be in God's grace, a question with no safe answer, she gave a reply that astonished the court.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>If I am not, may God put me there; and if I am, may God so keep me.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">The record of her trial, session of 24 February 1431.</p><div class="sp-full"><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“If I am not, may God put me there; and if I am, may God so keep me.” — Joan of Arc, Joan of Arc before Her Judges" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-joan-of-arc-before-her-judges.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>She was condemned as a relapsed heretic and burned in the marketplace of Rouen on 30 May 1431. Charles VII's armies went on to drive the English from Normandy and Gascony, ending the Hundred Years' War in 1453, and a retrial authorised by the pope declared her innocent in 1456. She was canonised in 1920.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a></div></div></section>
